<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artsakh (historical province)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artsakh was a province of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, corresponding broadly to the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>. After the partition of Armenia between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> around 387 it passed with neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utik">Utik</a> to Caucasian Albania, but its population remained closely tied to Armenian culture and the Armenian Church. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was ruled by Armenian princely houses such as that of Khachen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artsakh-historical-province.jpg" alt="Artsakh (historical province)" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original author: User Hovik95, I colored Artsakh green. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artsakh_within_Armenian_Kingdom.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
